narrative:
## Building Six Power Tools

You'll build one function from each major category and demonstrate them all.

### Math Tool: Absolute Value

```xiom
fn absolute(n: Int) -> Int {
  return math.abs_int(n);
}
```

### Math Tool: Minimum of Two

```xiom
fn minimum(a: Int, b: Int) -> Int {
  return math.min_int(a, b);
}
```

### String Tool: Shout (Uppercase)

```xiom
fn shout(text: Str) -> Str {
  return string.str_upper(text);
}
```

### Boolean Tool: In Range

```xiom
fn in_range(value: Int, low: Int, high: Int) -> Bool {
  return value >= low and value <= high;
}
```

### Random Tool: Dice Roll

```xiom
fn roll_dice() -> Int {
  return math.random_range(1, 6);
}
```

### Void Tool: Print Header

```xiom
fn print_header(title: Str) {
  io.println("=== DEMO COMPLETE ===");
}
```

### The Showcase

```xiom
fn main() -> Int {
  io.println("--- FUNCTION SHOWCASE ---");

  let abs_val = absolute(-42);

  let min_val = minimum(15, 8);

  let loud = shout("hello");

  let check1 = in_range(5, 1, 10);
  let check2 = in_range(15, 1, 10);

  let dice1 = roll_dice();
  let dice2 = roll_dice();

  print_header("All functions demonstrated!");

  return 0;
}
```

Six functions. Four categories. One main() that calls them all. This is the pattern for every program you'll ever build — specialized functions orchestrated by a clear main().

